A||N||3||Standard||Idle Fluctuations||||5
Z||000000||Standard||09-14-1999||01:40 AM||||My 84 2m4 idles at about 600rpm in drive, except when I have the turn signal on.  When the signal comes on, the revs drop; when it turns off, the revs come back up.  looks like it drops about 100rpm.  The volt meter swings back and forth with the signal on, also.  I'm thinking that something is sucking up too much power, or maybe it could be dirty connections?  I'm gonna take Cliff's advice and scrub off the posts.. any other ideas would be helpful.  Thanks||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||09-14-1999||08:51 AM||||same answer as always....... check all the ground links in the engine compartment. and thuroghly check both battery cables especially the thin leads off the battery mains.||||reg||2
Z||000002||Cooter||09-14-1999||09:24 PM||||When the alternator was going out on my car, it would do about the same thing, but it was still putting out enough power to keep the charge light from coming on. It was not until after I replaced the battery that the alternator finally let go. If it is the alternator, consider going back with one that has a higher amperage output. On my Surburban, I swapped the weak 54 amp for a 94 amp- the largest that still has the same case size. The higher output alternators usually live longer because they are not always running at maximum capacity. ||||reg||3
Z||000003||Standard||09-15-1999||12:37 AM||||I cleaned off the cables and grounds and it's doing better, still swings a little, tho.<BR>Is there supposed to be a lead on both the positive and negative battery posts?  My negative has one, but it looks like somebody cut the positive lead off, I can still see a little of that braided wire on the post.<P>Something in my engine compartment is making noise, and I think it's either the Alternator or water pump..  I replaced the battery about 2-3 months ago, and the alternator seems like it's doing fine.. I do want to get a 94 amp one, tho<BR>Thanks for the help!||||reg||11
